From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:07:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] UefiPayloadPkg: Arm the locks before code that is not the
 firmware's

coreboot's locks of the setup options and of the switch to the other
firmware slot are armed when a boot option is started, at ReadyToBoot,
so that the setup, which runs before, can change what they protect. The
boot manager runs other code before that: the option ROMs of the
devices, and the Driver#### and SysPrep#### load options, which an OS
creates with a write of a variable. With Secure Boot off, or signed,
such an image ran with the locks open and could write the options as
the setup does: BIOS Lock off for the next boot, from an OS that could
not turn it off itself. The events of the locks were registered once
the console was up, so an image started from a Driver#### option was
not even locked out when it called ExitBootServices().

ThirdPartyImageLockLib, a Security2 handler in SecurityStubDxe after
DxeImageVerificationLib, sees every image that Secure Boot lets
through. Before ReadyToBoot, the first one that does not come from a
firmware volume makes it arm the two locks, by the SMIs of SmmStoreLib
and before the image is loaded, and signal a new event group,
gUefiPayloadThirdPartyImageEventGroupGuid. PlatformBootManagerLib
publishes the answer of the option lock on that event, as at
ReadyToBoot, with a second volatile variable, OptionLockEarly, that
tells the OS that it happened before the setup, and registers its
events before EndOfDxe, when no such image was loaded yet.
CfrSetupMenuDxe sets the trigger of its CFR_OPTFLAG_LOCK_AT_BOOT
options.

The setup then cannot save in that boot, and a restart changes nothing
where the image runs at every boot. So a boot that was asked to end in
the firmware setup (EFI_OS_INDICATIONS_BOOT_TO_FW_UI, which an OS sets
for "restart into the firmware settings") loads no image from outside
the firmware volumes until a boot option is started: the handler
refuses them, the locks stay open, and the setup saves. When a write of
an option fails after such an image, the setup says why and offers that
restart, which sets the request itself.

One kind of image is still loaded in such a boot, without arming
anything: the driver in the option ROM of a display controller, which
on a machine without integrated graphics is the screen of the setup. It
comes from the flash of a card in the machine, not from a variable, and
Secure Boot checks it when it is on. On a machine where a card has an
option ROM, of any kind, the setup saves only in such a boot.

An image loaded from a capsule before EndOfDxe is deferred by
SecurityStubDxe and does not get to the handler.

On a coreboot without vboot the events of the locks were not
registered at all, the option lock with them: they now are whenever
SMMSTORE is there.
